1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
signing up for a subscription took one click.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,720
For seconds, zero friction, canceling it

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:07,940
according to federal regulators took an Olympic obstacle course.

4
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,980
When a software engineer tries to cancel Amazon Prime,

5
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,700
they hit four pages, six clicks, and 15 separate options.

6
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:17,440
Each screen was engineered to convince you to stay.

7
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:20,520
According to the FTC, Amazon engineers had a secret

8
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,840
internal code name for that cancellation maze.

9
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:24,300
They called it the Iliad flow.

10
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:26,600
Yes, named after Homer's epic poem

11
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,400
about an agonizing 10-year war.

12
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:31,080
By the end of this video, you will see the exact blueprint

13
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,080
of how modern companies turn cancellation into a labyrinth

14
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:37,100
and the psychological trap you must avoid whenever you try to leave.

15
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:40,460
The FTC alleged this digital maze was completely deliberate.

16
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:44,160
Amazon settled the federal case for two and a half billion dollars.

17
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,320
A legal settlement is not a guilty verdict,

18
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,800
yet companies do not surrender billions to defend an innocent button.

19
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:51,860
So why build a labyrinth in the first place?

20
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:56,100
Why would executives pay top developers to make leaving as painful as possible?

21
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:59,580
Because on Wall Street, subscription companies are judged by one metric.

22
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:00,440
Monthly churn.

23
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:04,220
Churn is the percentage of customers who cancel their subscriptions every month.

24
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:08,180
Investors track that percentage the way emergency doctors watch a pulse monitor.

25
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,500
Think of a commercial bucket with a hole in the bottom.

26
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,000
You can pour in new customers,

27
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,400
but acquiring them requires millions in marketing.

28
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,920
Or you can simply plug the hole with a broken cancellation page.

29
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:23,300
Suppose a subscription software company loses 3% of its paying subscribers every 30 days.

30
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,420
3% sounds completely harmless.

31
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:25,880
Right?

32
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:30,580
Yet over 12 months, nearly a third of their total customer base simply evaporates.

33
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,780
Valuations drop, stock prices fall, boardrooms panic.

34
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,480
Now look at what happens when you make leaving painful.

35
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:41,480
When internal user experience designers begged corporate leadership to simplify the cancel flow.

36
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:42,600
Executives refused.

37
00:01:42,980 --> 00:01:43,640
They said no.

38
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:45,960
They laughed at the idea of effortless cancellation.

39
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:46,600
Why?

40
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,680
Because every barrier protected millions in free cash flow.

41
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:51,420
Add a desperate discount pop-up.

42
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:52,500
Add an, are you sure?

43
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:53,040
Survey.

44
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:54,920
Add a hidden confirmation checkbox.

45
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:59,120
Every extra screen you click through is a point where exhausted users surrender.

46
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,020
The customer churned curved flatens.

47
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:02,420
The software didn't improve.

48
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:03,840
Your needs didn't change.

49
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:07,780
Yet on the corporate balance sheet, the company suddenly looks wildly profitable.

50
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,420
Notice that discounted rate that magically appears the moment you try to leave.

51
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,860
That lower price existed the entire time.

52
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:16,640
They just refused to give it to their most loyal customers.

53
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:21,480
Every confusing button is a quiet interest-free loan taken directly from your bank account.

54
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:22,680
Did you keep the subscription?

55
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:24,000
Because you love the service?

56
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,640
No, you kept it because life got busy and you simply gave up.

57
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,360
So you would assume someone in power would step in and ban this practice, right?

58
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,440
Regulators tried.

59
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:36,580
In 2024, the FTC finalized the federal click to cancel rule.

60
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,160
The rule was beautifully simple.

61
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:42,700
If you can sign up online with one click, you must be able to cancel online with one click.

62
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,620
Corporate lobbying coalitions immediately filed lawsuits.

63
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:50,100
An eighth circuit federal appeals court vacated the rule, throwing it straight into the trash.

64
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:51,720
So did the corporate maze win.

65
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:52,720
Not entirely.

66
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:56,160
Federal regulators simply pivoted to an older federal consumer law.

67
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:59,280
Rosca, the Restore Online Shoppers Confidence Act.

68
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:04,240
The law mandates that online merchants must provide simple, transparent cancellation mechanisms.

69
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:09,840
This fall, the FTC and 20 states filed a massive federal lawsuit against toast, the dominant

70
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,280
restaurant software provider.

71
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:16,440
Regulators alleged toast locked small restaurants into multi-year contracts with brutal cancellation

72
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:21,240
hurdles, charging early termination penalties, up to $150 per remaining month.

73
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:25,820
But toast aggressively disputes the lawsuit, arguing that consumer protection statutes do

74
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:28,100
not apply to negotiated business contracts.

75
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:30,380
Will small businesses get their freedom back?

76
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:32,320
A federal judge will decide the outcome.

77
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:36,960
Yet while federal rules stall in court, states like California, New York, and Illinois

78
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,280
enforce strict automatic renewal statutes, which means whether your wallet is protected,

79
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:43,580
often depends entirely on your zip code.

80
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,600
Now look at how other nations solved this exact headache.

81
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,440
In India, regulators didn't file 20-year lawsuits.

82
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:52,240
They changed the technical plumbing of the banking system.

83
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:57,000
The Reserve Bank of India enforced the mandatory E-mandate framework for credit cards.

84
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:02,380
Banks are required by law to send an SMS alert 24 hours before every recurring subscription

85
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:02,940
charge.

86
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:08,260
For transactions over 15,000 rupees, the bank cannot deduct a single rupee without two

87
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:09,360
factor authentication.

88
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,740
Global tech giants protested loudly claiming consumer friction would devastate their

89
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:14,720
subscription businesses.

90
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:16,140
Yet did commerce collapse?

91
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:17,320
Not for a second.

92
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:18,880
It destroyed corporate inertia.

93
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,480
In the United States, the default rule is simple.

94
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,020
Companies charge first and you must chase them down.

95
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:28,000
In India, the law forced companies to ask permission before touching your money.

96
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:30,140
Here is what the forensic paper trail reveals.

97
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:32,100
You are not fighting a bad website.

98
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:36,340
You are fighting an engineered behavioral trap, tuned by rooms full of data scientists.

99
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:40,520
Sign up flows are tested down to the millisecond to remove every hesitation.

100
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:44,320
And cancellation flows are engineered with friction to break your willpower.

101
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:47,580
That $12 zombie subscription isn't just taking your cash.

102
00:04:47,860 --> 00:04:52,000
It is extracting cognitive bandwidth, taxing you with monthly guilt and dread every time

103
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:52,960
you look at your statement.

104
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:56,480
If you have ever felt exhausted trying to cancel, do not blame yourself.

105
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:57,480
You are not lazy.

106
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:00,160
Your exhaustion was budgeted into their quarterly earnings.

107
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:02,860
The subscription economy never sold you convenience.

108
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:06,900
It bought permanent access to your bank account, betting you would be too tired to fight

109
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:07,240
back.

110
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:09,580
Next time, don't hand over your primary card.

111
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:11,280
Use single use virtual limits.

112
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:14,680
Break the pipeline because canceling isn't just saving $12.

113
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:17,100
It is evicting an algorithm from your wallet.

